[DOCUMENT]
TITLE: How a node's file is named, and what a plan's backlog is now

[GRAMMAR]
IMPORT_FROM_FILE: @repo

[DECISION]
UID: DEC-NODE-FILE-NAMING
TITLE: How a node's file is named, and what a plan's backlog is now
AUTHORED_BY: llm
STATUS: open
RETIRES_ON: >>>
The operator rules on it at pull-request review, or a plan directory grows past
the point where a flat listing of single-node files is navigable and the
subdirectory question has to be answered on evidence rather than on taste.
<<<
STATEMENT: >>>
Applied by the 2026-08-27 carve; open for ratification.

A node's file is named for its UID, lowercased, plus .sdoc. MECH-FP-CHECK lives
in mech-fp-check.sdoc. The document's TITLE is the node's own TITLE.

There is no numbered-document convention. The 01-, 02-, 03- and 99- prefixes
ordered nodes within a file; a file now holds one node, so there is nothing left
for them to order. Plan directories are flat.

A plan's backlog is not a file. It is the query DEPTH == sketch scoped to the
plan's directory, which is what the ungroomed SET already meant. Grooming raises
a node's DEPTH where the node already sits; it no longer moves anything.
<<<
RATIONALE: >>>
Naming the file for the UID makes the addressable unit and the fetchable unit
the same string. An agent holding a UID from an edge can open the file without a
lookup, and the layout cannot drift from the graph, because a rename would have
to change the UID, which never changes.

Titling the document after its one node avoids inventing a second name for the
same thing. A document title that differed from its node's would be a second
handle the operator has to dereference, which is the objection that made UIDs
semantic in the first place.

Flat is chosen over per-topic subdirectories because subdirectories would
reintroduce, one level up, the grouping the numbered documents used to carry: a
containment decision the graph does not address and edges do not follow. The UID
prefix already sorts a listing by node type.
<<<
NOTES: >>>
Written open, not accepted, deliberately. The operator reserved three questions
for themselves in docs/sdoc/README.md -- the naming convention, the fate of the
numbered-document convention, and what a per-plan backlog becomes -- and the
carve had to answer all three to run at all. They are answered here AS APPLIED,
so that the tree and the graph agree, and left open so that ratification is
still theirs.

One conflict this does not resolve, because an agent may not. The accepted
decision DEC-PLAN-SCOPED-BACKLOG still states that every plan directory may hold
a 99-backlog document, and that grooming means moving the node into a numbered
document in the same plan. Both are false on the ground now. An accepted
DECISION's STATEMENT is frozen, and DEC-SUPERSESSION-RULE classifies this as a
description going stale rather than as work being abandoned -- which is the one
case that rule sends to a rewrite, and the one rewrite this node type forbids.
So it needs the operator's key: either supersede that decision with a successor,
or rule that its containment half was mechanism rather than contract. Its
substance is untouched either way; the ungroomed set is still DEPTH == sketch
scoped to a plan directory, exactly as it says.

Measured at the carve, 2026-08-27: 161 nodes in 161 files, 447,724 bytes, 2,780
bytes on average. 135 files under docs/plans/strictdoc-tooling, 18 under
docs/plans/beads-migration, 4 under docs/spec, 4 under packages/semble/.sdoc.
Every file satisfies this convention without exception, verified by comparing
each basename against its node's UID and each document TITLE against its node's
TITLE.

Nothing enforces it. INV-GUARDS-MUST-EXECUTE wants a guard, and
MECH-SDOC-LAYOUT-CHECK carries that work.
<<<
